<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian War of 1499–1504</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1499">1499</a>–<a href="/years/1504">1504</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Second Italian War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian War of 1499–1504 was fought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a>, who conquered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1499 and then shared the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> with Ferdinand of Aragon. The partners fell out, and the Spanish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> defeated the French at Cerignola and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 1503. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1504 left Naples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Milan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1499-1504.jpg" alt="Italian War of 1499–1504" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toni Pecoraro · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battaglia_di_Cerignola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war followed the Italian War of 1494–1498, which began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Charles took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> with ease, but after he went home Ferdinand II of Naples won it back with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>. Charles died on 7 April 1498 and was succeeded by Louis XII, who inherited the Angevin claim to Naples and claimed Milan through his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentina-visconti">Valentina Visconti</a>. By the Treaty of Blois of 9 February 1499 he allied with the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which was promised Cremona and lands east of the Adda, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> approved the invasion in return for French backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>French troops under Gian Giacomo Trivulzio overran the duchy, Ludovico fled to Emperor Maximilian, and the French entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 6 October 1499. With 20,000 mercenaries Ludovico retook the city on 5 February 1500, but he could not pay them, his army was destroyed at the Battle of Novara in April, and his own men betrayed him to the French, who held him until his death in 1508. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> of 11 November 1500 Louis and Ferdinand agreed to divide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and Frederick of Naples abdicated on 25 July 1501.</p><p>Disputes over the shared lands brought war between the partners in the second half of 1502. Gonzalo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> at first avoided battle, and the fighting became a matter of skirmishes, among them the Challenge of Barletta on 13 February 1503. Helped by supplies brought by the Spanish navy and by quarrels between the French and Italian knights, the Spanish won at Cerignola on 28 April 1503. On 29 December they surprised the French army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> while it rested after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and destroyed it, killing about 4,000 of just over 15,000 soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Louis withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> on 2 January 1504, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, signed on 31 January, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Spain Sicily and the south; Milan stayed in French hands for thirteen years. A second treaty of Blois, of 22 September 1504, proposed to marry Louis's daughter Claude to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> with Milan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in her dowry, but the union never took place. The next phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cambrai">War of the League of Cambrai</a>, broke out in 1508.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian Wars of 1499–1504” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
